  Exact normal ratio midpoints are now excluded algebraically. A normalized
  binary64 denominator has at most 52 trailing zero bits. After 52 quotient
  bits, its remainder is divisible by that full power of two, while a midpoint
  remainder `D/2` has one fewer; an odd denominator has no integral midpoint
  remainder. The defensive C midpoint branch therefore remains fail closed but
  is unreachable for valid ratio geometry.

  This also makes normalized exponent `e<=-54` unconditional: at `e=-54` the
  cubic atan error is below `1/(3*2^53)` ulp, smaller than the minimum
  non-midpoint separation `2^-54` ulp, and lower binades only improve the gap.
  The runtime now returns safe before the 52-step quotient-remainder loop for
  this entire range.

  At `e=-28`, the dynamic remainder rule has exact threshold `2/3` of the
  rounding cell. Two fixed non-dyadic vectors straddle it within `1/40000` ulp.
  At `e=-29`, a second pair straddles the next exact threshold `13/24` within
  `1/30000` ulp. Q1152 confirms all four final bits while each lower vector
  stays kernel-required and each upper vector resolves early.

  For `-53<=e<=-28`, denominator spacing can now be proved from the exact odd
  factor instead of replacing `D` by the loose bound `2^53`. Writing
  `D=2^t*d` with odd `d`, every non-midpoint is at least `1/(2d)` ulp away; for
  `m=-(2e+55)`, `d <= 3*2^(m-1)` therefore skips the quotient loop because the
  cubic error is strictly below `2^-m/3` ulp. The implementation strips powers
  of two by shifts only and retains the exact remainder gate otherwise. A
  fixed-seed 4,320-pair `Fraction` differential covers all 27 normal margin
  binades `e=-27..-53` with 160 independent 53-bit numerator/denominator
  geometries each and matches every C preclassifier status/result.

